297:The \emph{mutation graph} of a mutation class $\big [ Q \big ]$ has a vertex for each quiver in $\big [ Q \big ]$ and an (undirected) edge $R \mutation{i} S$ labeled $i$ between $R$ and $S$ whenever $\mu_i(R) = S$. A \emph{mutation cycle} is a closed walk where no two consecutive vertices (quivers) and edges (mutations) coincide. 
298:We will generally denote a mutation cycle by some initial quiver $Q$ and a reduced mutation sequence $\mathbf{i}$ such that $\mu_{\mathbf i}(Q) = Q.$
299:If~$Q$ has no isolated vertices, then any reduced mutation sequence $\mathbf i$ such that $\mu_{\mathbf i}(Q)=Q$ is a mutation cycle.
301:A mutation cycle is \emph{simple} if it only visits each quiver once; that is, all quivers in $\big [ Q \big ]_{\mathbf i}$ are distinct except for $Q = \mu_{\mathbf i}(Q)$ (cf.\ \cite[Definition~5.1]{LMC}). 
