644:\label{def:forks}
645:An abundant quiver $Q$ is called a \emph{fork} if there is a vertex $r$ (called the \emph{point of return}) such that the induced full subquiver formed by deleting $r$ is acyclic, and for every oriented path $i \rightarrow r \rightarrow j$ we have $$b_{ji} > \max(b_{ir}, b_{rj}).$$
647:The \emph{forkless} part of a mutation class $\big [Q\big ]$ is the set of quivers in the class which are not forks. 
652:\begin{definition}[{\cite[Definition~3.7]{warkentin2014exchange}}]
653:A quiver $Q$ is called a \emph{pre-fork} if there is a pair of vertices $k\neq k'$ such that 
656:    \item the full induced subquivers formed by deleting either $k$ or $k'$ are forks with common point of return $r$.
660:\begin{definition}
662:An acyclic quiver $Q$ is called a \emph{key} if there are a pair of vertices $k, k'$ such that 
665:\item the subquiver formed by deleting $k$ (resp. $k'$) is abundant acyclic.
670:Most forks are characterized by having a \emph{unique descent} and being vortex free (see \cite[Section 6]{LMC}).
671:One important property of a fork is that all mutations (except possibly the mutation at the point of return) again result in forks.
672:Every mutation applied to an abundant acyclic quiver will result in either a fork or another abundant acyclic.
673:Preforks (with keys) generalize these properties \cite{MR4695532}.
