818:\subsection{Reddening Sequences and Forks} \label{subsec:reddening-forks}
820:As each reddening sequence gives a mutation cycle, we next turn our attention to where reddening sequences can be found in the mutation graph. 
821:We show that it suffices to restrict to the forkless part (Definition~\ref{def:forks}).
823:\begin{lemma} \label{lem:reddening forkless part}
824:    Let $Q$ be a non-fork that admits a reddening sequence.
825:    Any reduced reddening sequence $\mathbf{N}$ of $Q$ does not pass through a fork.
826:    In other words, any reddening sequence can be reduced to one that is contained completely in the forkless part of the mutation graph.
830:    If $Q$ is mutation-finite or disconnected, then the result follows trivially as $Q$ is not mutation-equivalent to any forks.
831:    We then assume that $Q$ is mutation-infinite and connected, meaning that it is mutation-equivalent to a fork.
832:    Let $\mathbf{N}$ be any reduced reddening sequence of $Q$.
833:    Then $\mu_{\mathbf{N}}(Q)$ is isomorphic to $Q$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:reddening gives iso}.
834:    Since $Q$ is not a fork, neither is $\mu_{\mathbf{N}}(Q)$ a fork.
835:    If mutation along $\mathbf{N}$ produces a fork, then we know that further mutation will only produce forks by the Tree Lemma \cite[Lemma~2.8]{warkentin2014exchange}, as we are working with a reduced mutation sequence and can never pass through a point of return.
836:    Therefore, we cannot arrive at a fork during our reddening sequence, proving the desired result.
839:We can then utilize Theorem \ref{thm:muller redd} to show that all reduced reddening sequences behave in such a way: always passing through the forkless part.
841:\begin{proposition}\label{prop:reddening-forks}
842:    Let $Q$ be any quiver that admits a reddening sequence.
843:    Then any reduced reddening sequence $\mathbf{N}$ of $Q$ with associated permutation $\sigma$ is a---possibly trivial---conjugation of a sequence of mutations of non-forks, i.e., $\mathbf{N} = \mathbf{MS\sigma(\mathbf{M}^{-1})}$ for sequences of mutations $\mathbf{M}$ and $ \mathbf{S}$, satisfying the following conditions:
845:        \item $\mu_{\mathbf{M}}(Q)$ is not a fork;
847:        \item every quiver in $[\mu_{\mathbf{M}}(Q)]_\mathbf{S}$ is also not a fork;
849:        \item the mutation sequence $\mathbf{S}$ is a reddening sequence for $\mu_{\mathbf{M}}(Q)$.
855:    If $Q$ is not a fork, then Lemma \ref{lem:reddening forkless part} proves the result immediately.
856:    Assume then that $Q$ is a fork, and let $\mathbf{M}$ be the shortest sequence of mutations resulting in a non-fork.
857:    Since $\mathbf{N}$ is reduced, the Tree Lemma \cite{warkentin2014exchange} and Corollary \ref{cor:reddening gives iso} tell us that the reddening sequence must enter the forkless part somewhere along the way.
858:    Thus $\mathbf{N} = \mathbf{MX}$ for some mutation sequence $\mathbf{X}$, as $\mathbf{M}$ will be the reduced portion entering the forkless part.
859:    By a similar argument, we know that $\mathbf{N} = \mathbf{MSV}$, where $\mathbf{V}$ is reverse of the shortest mutation sequence taking $\mu_{\mathbf{N}}(Q)$ to the forkless part.
860:    Let $\sigma$ be the associated permutation of the reddening sequence $\mathbf N$.
861:    By Corollary \ref{cor:reddening gives iso}, we know that $\mathbf{V} = \sigma(\mathbf{M}^{-1})$.
862:    The only assertion remaining is to show that $\mathbf{S}$ is a reddening sequence for $\mu_{\mathbf{M}}(Q)$ that remains in the forkless part.
865:    Thus Theorem \ref{thm:muller redd} tells us that $\mathbf{S}$ must be a reddening sequence for $\mu_{\mathbf{M}}(Q)$. Lastly,  
866:    Lemma~\ref{lem:reddening forkless part} proves our two assertions on $\mathbf{S}$.
869:\begin{remark} \label{rmk:reddening-pre-forks}
870:    A similar result holds for the case of pre-forkless part.
871:    This can be used to show that some quivers with a finite pre-forkless part, like Figure \ref{fig:box quiver}, do not admit a reddening sequence.
