# Mutation cycles from reddening sequences — verified generator

| Profile field | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | **B** — an efficient exact algorithm exists and is reported |
| Native domain | combinatorics |
| Object regime | finite discrete |
| Computational core | other (weighted-quiver mutation) |
| Certificate | exact symbolic reduced mutation word |
| Intended intuition | change of variables: conjugate an acyclic source cycle |
| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction or surrogate |

## Problem and trust model

The generator turns Ervin and Neville's [*Mutation Cycles from Reddening
Sequences*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.06573) into an exact witness task.  A solver
receives a labelled 4-by-4 integer exchange matrix and must return a reduced
vertex word of a specified length whose quiver mutations return the matrix
exactly.  `verify` checks shape and reducedness and replays Definition 2.2 with
integers; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.

Generation is inverse/theorem-backed.  It samples an abundant acyclic
three-vertex quiver, chooses the alternating conjugator
`M=(middle, endpoint)^q`, mutates that block, adjoins a fourth source, and carries
the known word `4, M^-1, S, M` through a random relabelling.  Theorem 4.21 proves
closure, Theorem 4.23 proves simplicity, and Theorem 4.27 proves that in this
rank-four regime it is the unique simple cycle.

## Why Track B

This is not a Track-A complexity claim.  Lemma 3.11 identifies the easy core:
an abundant acyclic quiver's unique reduced reddening sequence is its source
sequence.  Mechanically, the reference algorithm finds the unique decreasing
mutation of the cyclic rank-three block at every step, reaches that acyclic
core, takes its source order, and retraces the descent.  It is
`O(L r^3)` exact arithmetic (`r=4`); at shipping size it performs **24 trial
mutations and 329 conservatively counted scalar operations per instance** and
solves 8/8.  The measured eight-instance run is recorded in
`selftest_report.json`.

The compact route notices that the lightest and heaviest edges of the cyclic
rank-three block share its middle vertex.  The light edge identifies the
alternating conjugator, its direction identifies the source order, and `L`
fixes the repetition count.  That route uses 14 comparisons/arithmetic
operations.  Without the invariant, the solver must carry out the exact descent
on large integers; this mechanical-versus-compact gap is the Track-B claim.

## Worked demo

For `make_instance(n=3, repetitions=1, seed=0)`, the complete matrix is

```text
B =
  [0,-84,-7,747]
  [84,0,-10,-9]
  [7,10,0,11]
  [-747,9,-11,0]
L = 8
```

The answer is `<answer>[2,1,3,0,3,1,3,1]</answer>`, and `verify` returns
`(True, "ok")`.  Dropping the last entry returns
`(False, "wrong length: expected 8, received 7")`.  This smallest setting is
hand-solvable: eight exact mutations can be checked directly, and the invariant
reduces it to identifying one light edge.

