174-Mutation cycles are useful for a few reasons. %, we briefly mention some potential applications.
175-First, mutation cycles give nontrivial automorphisms in their cluster algebras; indeed, they are a necessary condition for the cluster automorphism group~\cite{AssemShifflerShramchenkoClusterAuts} (or cluster modular group~\cite{FockGoncharovEnsembles}) to be non-trivial.
176-%Second, it is often convenient to work with the smallest or simplest quiver in a mutation class (say, with the fewest number of arrows); mutation cycles often have multiple ``local minima''.
177-Second, understanding the possible mutation cycles can help us design and test algorithms for mutation equivalence. %Kinda wanna say something about detecting vortices for COQ stuff.
178-Detecting the mutation equivalence of quivers is a major open problem. 
179-Third, one can use mutation cycles to create discrete time dynamical systems (e.g.\ by specializing cluster variables and iterating the cluster automorphism from the cycle, or by embedding the quivers into larger quivers~\cite{MachacekOvenhouseDynamicalMutation}).
180-
181-Reddening sequences have also found several applications \cite{KellerGreenSurvey}.
182-For example, they give precise formulas for Donaldson-Thomas invariants \cite{kellerDT, KontsevichSoibelmanDT}, and, when a quiver has multiple reddening sequences, they provide quantum dilogarithm identities.
183-The automorphisms arising from reddening sequences (called twists) are often useful.
184-
185-
186-
187-All of the quivers on the mutation cycles we construct have reddening sequences of their own, and so may be used to construct yet more mutation cycles. 
188-Many of these quivers are locally-acyclic (in fact, Banff), and so in particular their upper cluster algebra agrees with their cluster algebra \cite{muller_locally_2013}.
189:This construction includes the constructions in both Theorem~1.1 and Example~10.3 of \cite{LMC}. 
190-%Many quivers resulting from our construction will contain \emph{vortices} (\cite[Definition~6.2]{LMC}), and so are not mutation acyclic.
191-Unlike the cycles constructed there, some of these mutation cycles may be `paved' by shorter mutation cycles.
192-Also, these mutation cycles are often not unique in the mutation class - indeed, if one of the quivers used in the triangular extension has multiple reddening sequences then the triangular extension will lie on multiple mutation cycles.
193-Our results also give a plethora of examples of mutation cycles, with many free parameters, and without needing an explicit description of all the quivers on the mutation cycles.
194-
195-Motivated by classifying mutation cycles, we also investigate properties of reddening sequences. 
196-We show that any reddening sequence must pass through the forkless part of the mutation graph, and is always conjugate to a reddening sequence entirely in the forkless part. 
197-Using these results, we classify all of the reddening sequences for abundant-acyclic quivers and keys.
198-These classifications also explicitly identify the isomorphism associated to the reddening sequences.
